  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n00z guidance is in much better agreement than the 18z guidance,\nwith the GFS coming into line with the ECMWF and Canadian\nsolutions for the extended period. The pattern looks to become\nactive, with increasing showers and thunderstorms for mid to\nlate week.\n\nMonday through Tuesday...the upper trough over the Pacific\nNorthwest will eject eastward across Montana into the Northern\nPlains early this week. Expect another round of showers and\nthunderstorms across the higher terrain Monday afternoon, then\nspreading east into the Plains during the evening and overnight\nhours. Lightning, gusty outflow winds to 45 mph and locally\nheavy rainfall will all be possible. A couple of storms that\nfire off during the late afternoon to early evening near the\nKansas border could become strong, with half inch hail and winds\nnear 50 mph. SPC did pull the marginal risk east into Kansas and\nout of our southeast Colorado Plains. Afternoon high\ntemperatures will remain hot, with mid 80s to mid 90s across the\nlower elevations. A cold front arrives overnight Monday, which\nwill bring cooler temperatures to the area by morning. Overnight\nlows by Tuesday morning will mainly be in the 50s across the\nPlains.\n\nBy Tuesday, low level flow turns southeasterly across the Plains\nand dewpoints rise back into the 50s, with near 60 degrees near\nthe Kansas border. Meanwhile, the next upper low takes shape\nacross the Pacific Northwest, with continued southwest flow\noverhead. An embedded vort max is forecast to lift northeast \nout of the Desert Southwest with a bit more shower and \nthunderstorm coverage across the Mountains. PWAT values begin to\nincrease and so does the heavy rain threat for area burn scars.\nThe main upper vort max lifts northeast Tuesday night, with \nthunderstorms forecast to develop across the Southeast Plains \nduring the evening and clear into Kansas overnight. Temperatures\non Tuesday look nice, with mostly 70s across the region.\n\nWednesday through Friday...models have come into agreement with\na slow moving upper low in the Pacific Northwest and building\nhigh pressure over the southeast states for mid to late week.\nThis will be deep southerly flow across Colorado, with multiple\nembedded waves lifting north out of the Desert Southwest. PWAT\nvalues continue to increase and dewpoints remain elevated in the\nupper 50s to lower 60s. Daily widespread showers and\nthunderstorms are expected across the whole area. Locally heavy\nrainfall and flash flooding on area burn scars will become\nelevated during this period and will need to be monitored.\nTemperatures during this period look to remain pleasant, with\n70s to lower 80s forecast across the lower elevations.\n\nSaturday into Sunday...the upper low tracks east across Montana\ninto the Northern Plains by next weekend. This will shift the\nmoisture plum east as upper ridging and drier air move across\nColorado. Shower and thunderstorm activity looks to become more\nscattered in nature. Temperatures will also warm a few degrees\nover the weekend.",
